do you really need to place a last comma in json request body?: height: roundedY,
}
};
No, the last comma is not necessary in the JSON request body. The last comma in an object literal is optional. It is used to separate multiple properties within the object, but it is not required after the last property.

Thus, in your JSON request body, you can remove the last comma after the height property:


const requestBody = {
stride: “1”,
nerdcodes: genRanNum(),
inputs: inputText,
parameters: {
guidance_scale: CFG,
negative_prompt: inputText2,
width: xxxInput,
height: yyyInput
}
};



It’s good practice to ensure valid JSON syntax by only including commas between the properties that have subsequent properties within the same object.

